{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Why, YHWH, does Your anger burn</b> — Behold, in this great sin it would have been fitting for Moses to pray by way of confession and supplication, as in \"Oh, this people has sinned a great sin\" (Exodus 32:31), and as Ezra prayed and confessed concerning the transgression of the exile (Ezra 10:1). And there is no reason for him to say \"Why, YHWH.\" And our Rabbis (Shemot Rabbah 43:7, 9) awaken on this matter, by way of the aggadot, to mention several reasons to minimize the sin before Him. And by way of truth, he says: Why do You give permission to the attribute of judgment to hold sway over Your people? For You brought them out for Your name's sake from the land of Egypt with the attribute of mercy upon them, and with the attribute of judgment upon their enemies. And this prayer — \"Why, YHWH\"; \"Why should the Egyptians say\" — it appears in truth that this prayer is the one he mentioned in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 9:26), \"And I prayed to YHWH and said: Adonai Elohim, do not destroy Your people,\" for the substance of the prayer is identical in both. Now, here he mentioned the prayer before his descent from the mountain, and there he mentioned it after his descent from the mountain.\n\nAnd the opinion of R. Abraham ibn Ezra is that Moses did not pray on behalf of Israel all the while that idolatry was among them, but when He said to him \"Leave Me and I will destroy them\" (Deuteronomy 9:14), he understood that the matter depended on him, and he went down and burned the calf and returned to pray for forty days — and there is no chronological earlier or later in the Torah. But my opinion is not so, because the prayer he made upon his return is the one he recounts: \"Oh, this people has sinned a great sin\" (Exodus 32:31). And if it is all one prayer that he made during the forty days after his return to the mountain, why would he divide it, mentioning part of it here (Exodus 32:11–13) and after the descent (Exodus 32:31) mentioning the other part (Exodus 32:31)? Rather, they are two prayers. Therefore it appears that when He said to him \"Let Me be, that My anger may burn against them and that I may consume them,\" immediately he appeased the countenance of HaShem and did not delay at all, for he feared lest the wrath go forth from before YHWH and the plague begin to consume them that very instant, and immediately he said: \"Why, YHWH, does Your anger burn against Your people.\" And thus I found in Shemot Rabbah (42:1): Moses said, \"If I leave Israel and go down, Israel has no standing in the world, but I am not moving from here until I seek mercy for them.\" Immediately Moses began to plead their defense, etc. And so he prayed for them, and HaShem relented from the evil that He had spoken of killing them and consuming them — not that He became reconciled to them, but rather He said \"I have relented; I will not make an end of them.\" And since he now had opportunity, Moses went down and burned the calf and killed those who had served it, and afterward he said to the people: \"I will go up to YHWH; perhaps I may make atonement for your sin\" (Exodus 32:30) — that He might forgive you.\n\nBut in Mishneh Torah he recounted the matter in a different order, for after the word of YHWH that He spoke — \"Leave Me and I will destroy them\" — he said \"And I turned and came down\" (Deuteronomy 9:26). And the reason is that there Moses was setting out for them all their sins and the burden he bore upon them. So he recounted what they did at Horeb with the calf (Deuteronomy 9:12–16), and that he had needed to shatter the tablets (Deuteronomy 9:17), and to pray for them forty days and forty nights (Deuteronomy 9:18–19), and also for Aaron (Deuteronomy 9:20), and that he toiled to burn the calf (Deuteronomy 9:21), and he recounted what they did at Taberah and at Massah and at Kivrot HaTa'avah (Deuteronomy 9:22), \"and when YHWH sent them from Kadesh Barnea\" (Deuteronomy 9:23). And after he concluded, \"You have been rebellious against YHWH from the day I knew you\" (Deuteronomy 9:24), he returned (Deuteronomy 9:25–29) to the matter of his prayers which he had mentioned, and arranged the two prayers and said: \"And I fell down before YHWH the forty days and the forty nights that I fell down, because YHWH had said to destroy you, and I prayed to YHWH and said: Adonai Elohim, do not destroy Your people,\" etc. — as if to say: I needed to fall down before YHWH on your behalf for forty days, because at first YHWH said to destroy you, until I prayed to Him: do not destroy Your people. And there was no need to mention the second prayer, for he had already said that he fell down before Him for forty days over them — for even here he did not mention it, for who could write how many supplications and entreaties he offered over them for forty days?\n\nAnd if you understand what I have explained, you will truly recognize that there were two prayers: at first he appeased the countenance of YHWH his Elohim (Exodus 32:11), and in the end he fell down before YHWH for forty days, as will be explained in the section of \"And Moses said to YHWH\" (Exodus 32:31). And he mentioned in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 9:20), \"And with Aaron YHWH was very angry, and I prayed also for Aaron at that time\" — and he did not mention this here at all, and this was for Aaron's honor, for Moses did not wish to mention in his lifetime that he had needed his prayer, so that he would not be ashamed, and there, after his death, he told us the truth. And this is the correct order in these passages:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The citation in the Hebrew '(שמות ל\"ב:י\"ב-ט\"ז)' and subsequent parenthetical references in that cluster actually correspond to verses in Deuteronomy 9, not Exodus 32; Ramban here quotes Deuteronomy 9:12–16 and onward. The verse references as they appear in the source text have been retained but the book name rendered reflects Ramban's actual intent (Deuteronomy)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:19.265001901Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}